19:T61c,## The "is-a" test, and where it breaks

Inheritance models **is-a**: a `SavingsAccount` *is an* `Account`. Great when that's genuinely true and stable.

It breaks when you use it to **share behaviour** that varies. Behaviour bolted on by a base class is **fixed at compile time** and **can't be mixed**.

## The combinatorial explosion

Say robots can *walk* or *fly*. With inheritance: `WalkingRobot`, `FlyingRobot`. Now you need one that does **both**, single inheritance won't let `WalkingFlyingRobot` extend two classes, so you copy-paste code. Add *swim* and you need 2³ − 1 = 7 combination classes. This is the **class explosion**.

Other inheritance pains:

- **Fragile base class**, a change in the parent silently breaks children.
- **Tight coupling**, subclasses depend on the parent's internals.
- **No runtime change**, a `FlyingRobot` can never become a walker while running.

## Composition: has-a, and swappable

Give the robot a `Movement` **field** it delegates to:

```
class Robot {
    private Movement mover;
    void setMover(Movement m){ this.mover = m; }   // swap anytime
    void move(){ mover.move(); }                    // delegate
}
```

Now `Walk`, `Fly`, `Swim` are tiny independent classes. Mix them freely, **change them at runtime**, and adding `Dig` touches nothing else.

> This is literally the **Strategy pattern**, and the reason the Gang of Four open with: **“favour object composition over class inheritance.”** Reach for inheritance only for a true, stable *is-a*; reach for composition for behaviour that varies.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-foundations","activeLesson":"composition-over-inheritance"}],["$","$L18","composition-over-inheritance",{"lesson":{"id":"composition-over-inheritance","patternId":"lld-foundations","title":"Composition over Inheritance","kind":"concept","difficulty":"Core","statement":"Inheritance locks behaviour in at compile time and explodes combinatorially. Composition, “has-a”, keeps it swappable. Prefer it.","companies":["Amazon","Google","Meta","Atlassian"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"The inheritance trap","code":["class Robot          { void move(){} }","class WalkingRobot   extends Robot { void move(){ walk(); } }","class FlyingRobot    extends Robot { void move(){ fly();  } }","// need walk + fly?  extends BOTH → impossible"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Start with a Robot base class that declares move(). Reasonable so far.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"robot","name":"Robot","methods":["+ move()"],"x":1,"y":0.1},{"id":"walk","name":"WalkingRobot","methods":["+ move(): walk"],"x":0,"y":1},{"id":"fly","name":"FlyingRobot","methods":["+ move(): fly"],"x":2,"y":1},{"id":"both","name":"WalkingFlyingRobot","methods":["+ move(): ??"],"x":1,"y":2,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[{"id":"i1","from":"walk","to":"robot","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"i2","from":"fly","to":"robot","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"i3","from":"both","to":"walk","kind":"inherit"},{"id":"i4","from":"both","to":"fly","kind":"inherit"}],"shownClassIds":["robot"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["robot"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"WalkingRobot extends Robot and overrides move() to walk. FlyingRobot extends Robot and flies. Behaviour is baked into the type.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["robot","walk","fly"],"shownRelIds":["i1","i2"],"activeIds":["walk","fly"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":4,"narration":"Now you need a robot that BOTH walks and flies. It would have to extend two classes, single inheritance forbids it. So you copy-paste move() and duplicate logic.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["robot","walk","fly","both"],"shownRelIds":["i1","i2","i3","i4"],"activeIds":["both"]},"vars":[["problem","can’t extend two parents"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"Add “swim” and the combinations explode: 2³−1 = 7 classes for 3 behaviours. The hierarchy is rigid, duplicated, and can never change at runtime. Inheritance was the wrong tool.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["robot","walk","fly","both"],"shownRelIds":["i1","i2","i3","i4"],"activeIds":["both"]},"vars":[["3 behaviours","7 classes 😖"]]}]},{"label":"Compose behaviours","code":["interface Movement { void move(); }","class Walk implements Movement { ... }   // + Fly, Swim","","class Robot {","    Movement mover;","    void setMover(Movement m){ mover = m; }   // swap anytime","    void move(){ mover.move(); }              // delegate","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":4,"narration":"Flip it: a Robot HAS-A Movement, a field it holds, instead of BEING a kind of mover. move() just delegates to whatever mover it currently has.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"robot","name":"Robot","fields":["- mover: Movement"],"methods":["+ move()","+ setMover(m)"],"x":0,"y":0.95},{"id":"move","name":"Movement","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ move()"],"x":1.6,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"walk","name":"Walk","methods":["+ move()"],"x":1.2,"y":1.7},{"id":"fly","name":"Fly","methods":["+ move()"],"x":2,"y":1.7},{"id":"swim","name":"Swim","methods":["+ move()"],"x":2.8,"y":1.7}],"relations":[{"id":"agg","from":"robot","to":"move","kind":"aggregate","label":"mover","toMult":"1"},{"id":"cw","from":"walk","to":"move","kind":"implement"},{"id":"cf","from":"fly","to":"move","kind":"implement"},{"id":"cs","from":"swim","to":"move","kind":"implement"}],"shownClassIds":["robot"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["robot"]},"vars":[["has-a","mover: Movement"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Movement is a small interface. The hollow diamond marks aggregation: the Robot holds a Movement, but they’re independent objects.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["robot","move"],"shownRelIds":["agg"],"activeIds":["move"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"Walk, Fly and Swim each implement Movement, tiny, focused, independent. No base-class coupling.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["robot","move","walk","fly","swim"],"shownRelIds":["agg","cw","cf","cs"],"activeIds":["walk","fly","swim"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":6,"narration":"robot.setMover(new Fly()), and the SAME robot now flies. Swap behaviour at runtime, mix any combination, with zero new Robot subclasses.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["robot","move","walk","fly","swim"],"shownRelIds":["agg","cw","cf","cs"],"activeIds":["robot","fly"]},"vars":[["runtime swap","setMover(new Fly())"],["new classes","0"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"No explosion, no fragile base class, fully flexible. You just rediscovered the Strategy pattern, and why the rule is “favour composition over inheritance.”","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["robot","move","walk","fly","swim"],"shownRelIds":["agg","cw","cf","cs"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["= ","Strategy pattern"]]}]}]}}]]}]
